to a package-level `var`:\n\n```go\nvar knownAgents = map[string]string{\n    \"claude\":     \"claude\",\n    \"claudecode\": \"claude\",\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nThis is zero-cost to fix and eliminates real allocation pressure.\n\n**4. `lipgloss.NewStyle()` in loops (lines 166-167 of stats_render.go, line 259, line 323) -- Low-impact (nit)**\n\nThree locations create `lipgloss.NewStyle().Foreground(lipgloss.Color(display.Color))` inside loops. lipgloss style creation is cheap (it's a value type, no heap allocation in recent versions), and the loop counts are small (at most ~11 agents, ~5 repos, ~a few dozen commits). However, since the styles are fully determined by the `agentDisplayMap` (which is a package-level var), you could add a `Style lipgloss.Style` field to `agentDisplay` and initialize it once. That's cleaner code but not a performance problem.\n\n**5. String building in `normalizeAgentString` (lines 299-309) -- False-positive (skip)**\n\nThe `strings.Builder` + `WriteRune`/`WriteByte` approach is standard and efficient. A `strings.ToLower` + `strings.NewReplacer` alternative would be shorter but actually allocates more (NewReplacer creates internal structures, and you'd need two passes: replace then lower). The current implementation does a single pass. No change needed.\n\n**6. Duplicate agent-total derivation in `computeRepoContributions` vs `renderAgentBreakdown` (lines 212-237 and 144-151 of stats_render.go) -- Low-impact (nit)**\n\n`renderAgentBreakdown` re-aggregates agent totals from the repos that `computeRepoContributions` already grouped. You could return an `agentTotals map[string]int` alongside the repo list from `computeRepoContributions` and pass it through. It's a trivial amount of work (iterating ~5 repos, ~2-3 agents each), so this is a code-clarity nit, not a performance concern.\n\n**7. Memory / unbounded allocations -- False-positive (skip)**\n\nConfirmed: data is capped at `statsLimit = 500` from both endpoints. All derived structures (`byRepo`, `byDate`, `dateSet`, etc.) are bounded by the input size. No unbounded growth possible.\n\n---\n\n**Bug spotted (not in your list):**\n\nLine 327 of `stats_cmd.go`, the \"Factory AI Droid\" check:\n\n```go\nif len(lower) >= 15 && lower[:15] == \"factoryaidroid\" {\n```\n\n`\"factoryaidroid\"` is 14 characters, but the check requires `len(lower) >= 15` and takes `lower[:15]` (15 chars). This means the condition can never match the exact string \"factoryaidroid\" -- it would only match strings like \"factoryaidroidx\" (15+ chars where the first 15 start with \"factoryaidroid\" plus one more character). Fix: change to `len(lower) >= 14 && lower[:14] == \"factoryaidroid\"`, or simply use `strings.HasPrefix(lower, \"factoryaidroid\")`.\n\n---\n\n**Summary of recommendations:**\n\n| # | Finding | Rating | Action |\n|---|---------|--------|--------|\n| 1 | Sequential API calls | **High** | Use errgroup for concurrent fetches |\n| 2 | Triple iteration in computeContributionStats | Low | Merge into single pass |\n| 3 | Map allocation per call in normalizeAgentString | **High** | Hoist to package-level var |\n| 4 | lipgloss.NewStyle() in loops | Low | Pre-compute in agentDisplay |\n| 5 | String building approach | Skip | Current approach is fine |\n| 6 | Duplicate agent aggregation | Low | Compute once, pass through |\n| 7 | Unbounded memory | Skip | Properly bounded by statsLimit |\n| -- | Off-by-one bug in Factory AI Droid check | **Bug** | Fix length check from 15 to 14 |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af3fd1e4f3c43a8f0 (use SendMessage with to: 'af3fd1e4f3c43a8f0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 34958\ntool_uses: 5\nduration_ms: 45348</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"457bc781-4c09-4716-beb7-8be2c7ee5212","timestamp":"2026-04-21T14:39:47.213Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review the following new Go code for efficiency issues. The code is three new files for an `entire stats` CLI command in /Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments