{"parentUuid":"f5a8c5e0-e5db-44f0-8b50-bf8798355282","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UyBDAcsXUGvbDH4Bm7WuRq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question — the web version uses an SVG scatter plot with variable-radius circles, which doesn't translate directly to a terminal. But there are a few workable approaches:\n\n**Option A: Sized dot scatter** (closest to the web bubble chart)\nUse Unicode circle characters at varying sizes, colored by dominant agent:\n\n```\nCONTRIBUTIONS                                              500 checkpoints\n        Mar                        Apr                   Today\n 0  ·           ·                    ·     ·\n 6      •  ·        ·    ·               ·    •\n12    ●   •  ●  •  ●  •    ●   •  ●   ●  ●  ●   •\n18  •  ●  ●     •     ●  •  ●  •     •  ●  •  ●  ●\n        ·  = 1-2    •  = 3-5    ●  = 6+\n● Claude Code 36%  ● Codex 52%  ● OpenCode 1%\n```\n\nCharacters: `·` (1-2 steps), `•` (3-5), `●` (6+) — each colored by dominant agent.\nY-axis = hour of day (0, 6, 12, 18). One column per day (~30 cols for a month).\n\n**Option B: Braille scatter** (highest resolution)\nUnicode braille characters give 2x4 pixel resolution per cell — so a 40-column × 3-row area gives 80x12 effective dots. Can render actual dot positions with size implied by density. Looks slick but harder to color per-agent.\n\n**Option C: Block heatmap** (GitHub-style, simplest)\n```\nCONTRIBUTIONS\n     Mar                              Apr                Today\nMo   ░░▒▒░░▓▓░░  ░░▒▒██▓▓  ▒▒▓▓██████░░  ░░▒▒▓▓██\nTu   ▒▒▓▓██░░▒▒  ▓▓████▓▓  ████████████  ▒▒▓▓████\n...\n```\nIntensity = total steps, color = dominant agent. Compact but loses the hour-of-day axis.\n\nMy recommendation would be **Option A** — it's the closest match to the web UI, the hour axis is useful (shows work patterns), and the sized dots are readable even without color. It takes ~6 rows of vertical space which fits well above the repo chart in the fixed header.\n\nWant me to go with that approach?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":217,"cache_read_input_tokens":221492,"output_tokens":1420,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":217,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":1420,"cache_read_input_tokens":221492,"cache_creation_input_tokens":217,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":217},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaHB9RYLtgP1nx9qawA5W","type":"assistant","uuid":"78d9e143-ebdb-462e-9054-c6c9a41e56d7","timestamp":"2026-04-21T15:46:15.474Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments","sessionId":"06af762d-fb18-426b-8b89-e1a11aba933b","version":"2.1.104","gitBranch":"soph/stats","slug":"adaptive-wiggling-lemon"}
